<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemaic Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ptolemaic dynasty, Ptolemaic Empire, Ptolemaic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ptolemaic Kingdom was a Hellenistic state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> founded in 305 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s generals, after the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>. Ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, it combined Greek administration with pharaonic tradition. A long-standing rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, it ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> and Roman annexation in 30 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom.jpg" alt="Ptolemaic Kingdom" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaidor; derivative work by rowanwindwhistler; derivative work by Amphipolis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemaic_Kingdom_III-II_century_BC_-_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 332 BCE, when it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. He respected Egyptian religion, appointed Macedonians to almost all senior posts, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> as the new capital. After his death in 323 BCE the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> appointed Ptolemy, one of his closest companions, satrap of Egypt. Ptolemy defended Egypt against an invasion by Perdiccas in 321 BCE, gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>, and took the titles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basileus">basileus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> in 305 BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the mid third century BCE Ptolemaic Egypt was the wealthiest of the successor states. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a> the kingdom was the leading power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-wars">Syrian Wars</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> it held eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, the Sinai and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV Philopator</a> was a weak king, but his ministers won the victory of Raphia over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> in 217 BCE, while native Egyptian rebellions took over half the country for more than twenty years.</p><p>After the death of Ptolemy VI in 145 BCE, civil wars and feuds within the dynasty lasted for over a century and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> a de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Cleopatra VII, who came to the throne on 22 March 51 BCE, tried to restore Ptolemaic power, but Egypt was drawn into a Roman civil war and conquered by Rome after her death in 30 BCE, the last independent Hellenistic state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Ptolemies adopted the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> beside the Greek basileus and were shown on monuments in Egyptian style, while the monarchy kept its Hellenistic character. A bureaucracy extracted the country's wealth for a Greek ruling class that dominated military, political and economic affairs and rarely mixed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. Natives kept local and religious institutions and gained power in the central administration only if they Hellenized. Ptolemy I created the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a>, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apis">Apis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> with Greek deities, to win support from both peoples.</p><p>Royal officials regulated almost all economic activity, including the making of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, linen and beer. The Ptolemies drained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, built canals and minted coins in gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a> and the Museion, which drew scholars from across the Greek world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemaic Kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike the <b>Ptolemies</b> and Seleucids, the Antigonids ruled a largely Macedonian population and did not promote a royal cult at home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Sarmatian peoples pushed westwards across the steppe, the Scythians of the Crimea revived under kings such as Scilurus, and the Bosporan grain trade faced competition, which some historians link to exports from <b>Ptolemaic Egypt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death at the Battle of Corupedium in 281 BCE the old name returned, and Ephesus passed between the Seleucid Empire and the <b>Ptolemaic Empire</b> until Antiochus III took it in 197–196 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Interaction with Assyria, <b>Ptolemaic Egypt</b> and Rome placed Kush within a wider international system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His generals, the Diadochi, divided the empire through a series of wars (322–281 BCE), from which came the <b>Ptolemaic dynasty</b> in Egypt, the Seleucid Empire in Asia and the Antigonid kingdom in Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hellenistic kings Miletus was usually a nominally free city that passed between Antigonus, Lysimachus, the <b>Ptolemies</b> and the Seleucids; its citizens honoured Antiochus II with the title Theos, god, for removing the tyrant Timarchus around 259 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
